A||||7||batboy||exhaust manifold removal||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||03-08-2000||12:31 PM||||Just got back from the muffler shop. I had two exhaust leaks on my 85 GT, one was a weld that had failed on the cat replacement pipe. No problem fixing that one and it only cost $20, but the other leak is at the firewall side manifold, the gasket is leaking on the passenger side. The muffler shop declined to work on the manifold. Said it looked like to much work, but referred me to a guy that they farm out a lot of manifold work to, who supposedly has worked on Fieros before. I'm too busy working on my Batmobile to mess with this ding-dang manifold on my GT, so I'll have this mechanic look at it. For those of you that have pulled that firewall exhaust manifold off a Fiero V6 before, is there any special method that need to be followed? That manifold can be unbloted enough to slip a new gasket in, right? Any info will be appreciated...thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||03-08-2000||02:33 PM||||That shouldn't be a problem as long as the bolts don't break off.  I'm having the same problem with mine.  Given that these manifolds seem to be prone to cracking, it might be worth a few extra bucks to have him just put on a brand new one. That's what I plan to do with mine.  later...||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||03-08-2000||03:24 PM||||Batboy, probably not somthing you will try, but if/when my mainifolds ever need some work, I would suggest tilting the cradle down several inches. From the work you do, it shouldn't be a very big deal. Battery, shift cables, fuel lines. Those disconnected, and the cradle lowered, should allow a LOT more room back there. Just a suggestion, plus if any bolts break <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> in the head, lots more room to get them out. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||03-08-2000||03:51 PM||||There are excelent instructions for this at this link:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/exhaust-manifolds.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/exhaust-manifolds.html</A> <P> It is the Fiero Online Service page. May want to try to access it also this way; <A HREF="http://www.surf.to/FIERO_OSG" TARGET=_blank>http://www.surf.to/FIERO_OSG</A>  <BR>and then check for Exaust Manifolds. Hope it helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Capt Fiero||03-08-2000||04:31 PM||||How about a stripped out bolt hole in the head.  It is on the rear side.  It does not have a broken stud in it, it is just stripped out.  I think what happend is the shop that did the work about 3 years ago, installed the manifolds while the engine was on the ground and cross threaded it with air tools.  Then when I replaced the gasket about a month ago, the bolt was real hard to get out and now will not tighten back in.  As for replacing the rear manifold.  Just take your time, if you have AC remove it for the job.  Some bolts are easy to get from top some are easy to get from bottom.  So are just not easy from anywhere.  Only thing that I can say for sure is dissconnect it from the crossover pipe.  I have seen to many people try and just unbolt the 6 bolts and slide the new gasket it.  It makes it a ton harder to get everything lined back up when it is still being held by the crossover pipe.  It is a long hard job but it can be done with just a set of ramps and various sockets and ratchets.  Do not skimp out on gaskets, buy the best ones you can find.  You do not want to do this job twice.<BR>David||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-08-2000||04:56 PM||||Thanks for the info and links guys. I talked to the mechanic who the muffler shop referred me to...oh boy! He told me, sure he'd worked on plenty of Fieros. I told him we thought the exhaust manifold gasket was shot because we had it up on the rack and saw the gasket material was burnt away at one corner of the manifold. He proceeded to inform me it was a cracked manifold without even looking at it. I told him, yes they were prone to cracking and it might be cracked, but the muffler guy and I didn't <I>think</I> it was cracked from what we could see. Again he declared it was cracked and explained to me that those Fieros had aluminum heads(?) and cast iron manifolds(?) which caused differential heating, which cracks the manifold all the time. I told him to have a nice day and then I left. I sort of lost confidence in that guy!||||reg||1
Z||000006||doughboySE||03-08-2000||10:07 PM||||hey bat!!!!! i have the same problem when i had my new exhaust put on last year just after the car was painted the muffler man said that i had a leeking gasket at the fire wall manifol but being as 20 min before i had shelled out 1500.00 cash for the paint job i decided the wife would say no so i passed.<BR>he said it will be 250.00 to replace the gasket. can you hear a his behind you from in the car? and would this cause my baby to blow codes?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||03-08-2000||10:24 PM||||Batboy... Be sure to soak all the manifold fasteners with Liquid Wrench, or similar, before you start removing them. Let them soak for a while. Even overnight. A second application wouldn't hurt. <BR>I took off my exhaust crossover pipe last week to get a crack welded, and only had to grind off one bolt. And it was so rusted away that the socket wouldn't grip the head. Once I had the pipe off, the rest of the bolt came out fairly easily with vice-grips.  ||||reg||1
